<%# The keep control's per-visitor half. Locals: painting, kept, autofocus,
    compact: (default false — see below), count: (required unless compact)

    It carries its own <turbo-frame> rather than only the contents, because a
    frame-targeted response has to hand back a matching frame id or Turbo reports
    missing content.

    TWO ARCHITECTURES, ONE PARTIAL (story 0020). On `/` and `/days/:date` this
    frame is EAGER with a `src` — `daily/_day.html.erb` renders that wrapper
    because those pages are `Cache-Control: public` behind Thruster, and a real
    `button_to` there would mint a CSRF token, start a session, and put
    `Set-Cookie` on a page a shared cache serves to everyone. THE BOUNDARY THAT
    SPLIT SITS ON: the glyph is cacheable, the button is not — `_day` renders
    `_keep_glyph` directly as that frame's default content, and this partial,
    carrying the real button, lands a private moment later.
    `test/integration/favorites_test.rb` holds that half.

    `/feed` and `/artists/:slug` are walled and private — no shared cache to
    poison — so `paintings/_painting.html.erb` renders THIS partial inline, with
    no `src`, carrying the real button from the first paint. `compact: true`
    marks that path: it drops the "N kept" link below, because ten copies of it
    down one scroll is noise the compass's own `Kept` door already covers at
    zero (`decisions/0010`, narrowed 2026-08-19 — mark-only there deletes one of
    the two mitigations that decision bought the label-less rail with; the
    other, the accessible name below, stays whole on every surface).
    `test/system/feed_test.rb` and the artist-page tests hold that half.

    No visible words on the mark itself, so the `aria-label` is the whole
    announcement — see `decisions/0010-actions-become-a-rail.md`. `aria-pressed`
    is unchanged; it never depended on the label.

    `autofocus` is true ONLY on the write responses. Turbo replaces the whole
    frame on submit, which destroys the focused button and drops focus to <body>;
    autofocus puts it back, and focus landing on a toggle is also what makes a
    screen reader announce the new state. On the initial GET it is false — a
    fragment arriving unbidden must not steal focus, which is the same reason
    there is no aria-live here. %>
<%# No `class:` here, deliberately. Turbo replaces a frame by transplanting this
    element's CHILDREN into the one already on the page — the response frame's own
    attributes are discarded. The `.rail__slot` class lives on the frame in
    `daily/_day.html.erb`, which is the element that stays. Adding it here too
    looks load-bearing and is not. %>
<% compact = local_assigns.fetch(:compact, false) %>
<%= turbo_frame_tag "keep_#{painting.id}" do %>
  <%# The hidden `compact` field, not header/referer inference: the frame id
      `keep_<id>` is identical on every surface, and the referer is not a
      contract. An empty hash when not compact emits no field at all. %>
  <%= button_to favorite_path(painting), method: (kept ? :delete : :post),
        params: (compact ? { compact: "1" } : {}),
        form: { class: "rail__form" },
        class: "rail__act",
        autofocus: autofocus,
        aria: {
          pressed: kept,
          label: kept ? "Remove #{painting.title} from your collection"
                      : "Keep #{painting.title} in your collection"
        } do %><%= render "favorites/keep_glyph", filled: kept %><% end %>

  <%# The only word in the rail, and the reason it is a word — on `/` and
      `/days/:date` only. `3` alone would save nothing and throw away the one
      thing telling a first-time reader what the glyph beside it does — which
      matters more since the labels went (0010).

      Only once there is something to see. The compass's `Kept` covers discovery
      at zero; this one is the count climbing, which is the reward.

      `turbo_frame: "_top"` because a link inside a frame navigates the frame by
      default — without it, tapping this loads the whole collection page into a
      44px box and the reader goes nowhere.

      `count` is read ONLY inside this guard, and `compact` skips the guard
      entirely rather than defaulting `count` to `0`. A caller on the
      non-compact path that forgot to pass `count` gets a loud NameError; a
      silent `0` would be a wrong number on screen, which is worse — the exact
      failure this whole design exists to prevent. %>
  <% unless compact %>
    <% if count.positive? %>
      <%= link_to "#{count} kept", collection_path, class: "rail__count",
            data: { turbo_frame: "_top" } %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
